{
  "image_name": "1d2f8293a79604585b149f5253f28e4d.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a neuro-immune pathway linking the nervous system to inflammation in peripheral tissue. Sensory C fibres project through the dorsal root ganglion to the spinal cord and are shown under descending “cortical influences” from the brain, suggesting that central signals can modulate peripheral nerve activity. These peripheral nerve endings then release neuropeptides into local tissue.\n\nThe main visible consequence is a local inflammatory response. Neuropeptides act on blood vessels, causing vasodilation and oedema, and they also influence nearby skin and immune cells. A boxed tissue compartment shows keratinocytes, dendritic cells, and mast cells as major targets. These cells appear to respond by producing inflammatory mediators, including cytokines and histamine.\n\nThe downstream immune effect shown is T cell activation, with arrows from the local cellular response toward T cells. Overall, the diagram presents inflammation as a bidirectional neurocutaneous process: signals from the brain and spinal pathways affect peripheral sensory nerves, and those nerves release mediators that alter blood flow, increase swelling, and stimulate immune and skin-resident cells, amplifying local immune activation.",
